whoever said cartridge-based gaming is  dead is probably right but the classic  gaming brand koliko may resurface in the  near future with help from retro video  game systems incorporated the pair will  soon launch a new Kickstarter campaign  to fund the launch of the Calico  chameleon console which comes hot on the  heels of the failed IndieGoGo campaign  for the retro vgs console a near  identical product from retro video game  systems inc that failed to meet its  two-hundred-thousand-dollar goal last  month the chameleon is described as a  versatile new video game system that  serves as a modern-day take on the  classic gaming console it can play 8 16  and 32 bit games which koliko Holdings  says is a growing in popular genre  koliko holdings is a subsidiary of  riverwest brands who purchased the  rights to the koliko name from Hasbro  the Kaliko chameleon marks the first new  console that can play cartridges in two  decades Mike Kennedy retro video game  systems president describes it as a love  letter to all the classic  cartridge-based game systems that came  before he added it will take gamers and  their families back to a simpler time  when games were all about great gameplay  and fun factor the original colecovision  console was released in 1982 then the  original koliko company went bankrupt in  1988 and shortly after Hasbro acquired  most of his assets posting to Twitter  the developers assured fans and those  who are interested that it will be  significantly more affordable than the  first go around the Calico chameleon  will be available for purchase in early  2016 if the campaign is successful
 

